#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:45
msgid "A Simple Model of Solid Electrolyte Interphase (SEI) Growth"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:56
msgid ""
"The SEI is a porous layer that forms on the surfaces of negative electrode "
"particles from the products of electrochemical reactions which consume "
"lithium and electrolyte solvents. In the first few cycles of use, a lithium-"
"ion battery loses a large amount of capacity; this is generally attributed "
"to lithium being consumed to produce SEI. However, after a few cycles, the "
"rate of capacity loss slows at a rate often (but not always) reported to "
"scale with the square root of time. SEI growth is therefore often considered "
"to be limited in some way by a diffusion process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:69
msgid "Dimensional Model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:71
msgid ""
"In our simple SEI model, we consider a one-dimensional SEI which extends "
"from the surface of a planar negative electrode at :math:`x=0` until :math:"
"`x=L`, where :math:`L` is the thickness of the SEI. Since the SEI is porous, "
"there is some electrolyte within the region :math:`x\\in[0, L]` and "
"therefore some concentration of solvent, :math:`c`. Within the porous SEI, "
"the solvent is transported via a diffusion process according to:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:73
msgid ""
"\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial t} = - \\frac{\\partial N}{\\partial x}, "
"\\quad N = - D(c) \\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial x} \\label{1}\\\\"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:78
msgid ""
"where :math:`t` is the time, :math:`N` is the solvent flux, and :math:`D(c)` "
"is the effective solvent diffusivity (a function of the solvent "
"concentration)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:80
msgid ""
"On the electrode-SEI surface (:math:`x=0`) the solvent is consumed by the "
"SEI growth reaction, :math:`R`. We assume that diffusion of solvent in the "
"bulk electrolyte (:math:`x>L`) is fast so that on the SEI-electrolyte "
"surface (:math:`x=L`) the concentration of solvent is fixed at the value :"
"math:`c_{\\infty}`. Therefore, the boundary conditions are"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:82
msgid "N|_{x=0} = - R, \\quad c|_{x=L} = c_{\\infty},"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:87
msgid ""
"We also assume that the concentration of solvent within the SEI is initially "
"uniform and equal to the bulk electrolyte solvent concentration, so that the "
"initial condition is"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:89
msgid "c|_{t=0} = c_{\\infty}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:94
msgid ""
"Since the SEI is growing, we require an additional equation for the SEI "
"thickness. The thickness of the SEI grows at a rate proportional to the SEI "
"growth reaction :math:`R`, where the constant of proportionality is the "
"partial molar volume of the reaction products, :math:`\\hat{V}`. We also "
"assume that the SEI is initially of thickness :math:`L_0`. Therefore, we have"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:96
msgid "\\frac{d L}{d t} = \\hat{V} R, \\quad L|_{t=0} = L_0"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:101
msgid ""
"Finally, we assume for the sake of simplicity that the SEI growth reaction "
"is irreversible and that the potential difference across the SEI is "
"constant. The reaction is also assumed to be proportional to the "
"concentration of solvent at the electrode-SEI surface (:math:`x=0`). "
"Therefore, the reaction flux is given by"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:103
msgid "R = k c|_{x=0}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:108
msgid ""
"where :math:`k` is the reaction rate constant (which is in general dependent "
"upon the potential difference across the SEI)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:111
msgid "Fixing the moving boundary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:113
msgid ""
"The model above is a moving boundary problem as it is defined in :math:"
"`x\\in[0, L]`. In order to solve it, we need to fix the boundary by "
"introducing the scaling"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:115
msgid "x = L \\xi."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:120
msgid "Then, applying the chain rule we have"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:122
msgid ""
"\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x} \\rightarrow \\frac{1}{L} \\frac{\\partial}"
"{\\partial \\xi}, \\quad \\text{ and } \\quad \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t} "
"\\rightarrow \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t} - \\frac{L'(t)}{L(t)} \\xi "
"\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial \\xi}."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:127
msgid "Then, (1) can be rewritten as"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:129
msgid ""
"\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial t} = \\frac{\\hat{V} R}{L} \\xi "
"\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial \\xi} + \\frac{1}{L^2} \\frac{\\partial}"
"{\\partial \\xi} \\left( D(c) \\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial \\xi}\\right)"
msgstr ""

