#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:216
msgid "A model is defined in six steps:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:218
#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:237
msgid "Initialise model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:219
#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:270
msgid "Define parameters and variables"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:220
#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:337
msgid "State governing equations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:221
#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:399
msgid "State boundary conditions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:222
#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:511
msgid "State initial conditions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:223
#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:582
msgid "State output variables"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:225
msgid ""
"We shall proceed through each step to enter our simple SEI growth model."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:248
msgid ""
"We first initialise the model using the ``BaseModel`` class. This sets up "
"the required structure for our model."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[2]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "model = pybamm.BaseModel()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:281
msgid ""
"In our SEI model, we have two dimensionless parameters, :math:`k` and :math:"
"`\\hat{V}`, and one dimensionless function :math:`D(c)`, which are all given "
"in terms of the dimensional parameters, see (5). In pybamm, inputs are "
"dimensional, so we first state all the dimensional parameters. We then "
"define the dimensionless parameters, which are expressed an non-dimensional "
"groupings of dimensional parameters. To define the dimensional parameters, "
"we use the ``Parameter`` object to create parameter symbols. Parameters "
"which are functions are defined using ``FunctionParameter`` object and "
"should be defined within a python function as shown."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[3]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"# dimensional parameters\n"
"k = pybamm.Parameter(\"Reaction rate constant [m.s-1]\")\n"
"L_0 = pybamm.Parameter(\"Initial thickness [m]\")\n"
"V_hat = pybamm.Parameter(\"Partial molar volume [m3.mol-1]\")\n"
"c_inf = pybamm.Parameter(\"Bulk electrolyte solvent concentration [mol."
"m-3]\")\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"def D(cc):\n"
"    return pybamm.FunctionParameter(\n"
"        \"Diffusivity [m2.s-1]\", {\"Solvent concentration [mol.m-3]\": cc}\n"
"    )"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:313
msgid ""
"We now define the dimensionless variables in our model. Since these are the "
"variables we solve for directly, we do not need to write them in terms of "
"the dimensional variables. We simply use ``SpatialVariable`` and "
"``Variable`` to create the required symbols:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[4]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"xi = pybamm.SpatialVariable(\"xi\", domain=\"SEI layer\", "
"coord_sys=\"cartesian\")\n"
"c = pybamm.Variable(\"Solvent concentration [mol.m-3]\", domain=\"SEI "
"layer\")\n"
"L = pybamm.Variable(\"SEI thickness [m]\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:348
msgid ""
"We can now use the symbols we have created for our parameters and variables "
"to write out our governing equations. Note that before we use the reaction "
"flux and solvent flux, we must derive new symbols for them from the defined "
"parameter and variable symbols. Each governing equation must also be stated "
"in the explicit form ``d/dt = rhs`` since pybamm only stores the right hand "
"side (rhs) and assumes that the left hand side is the time derivative. The "
"governing equations are then simply"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[5]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"# SEI reaction flux\n"
"R = k * pybamm.BoundaryValue(c, \"left\")\n"
"\n"
"# solvent concentration equation\n"
"N = -1 / L * D(c) * pybamm.grad(c)\n"
"dcdt = (V_hat * R) / L * pybamm.inner(xi, pybamm.grad(c)) - 1 / L * pybamm."
"div(N)\n"
"\n"
"# SEI thickness equation\n"
"dLdt = V_hat * R"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:377
msgid ""
"Once we have stated the equations, we can add them to the ``model.rhs`` "
"dictionary. This is a dictionary whose keys are the variables being solved "
"for, and whose values correspond right hand sides of the governing equations "
"for each variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[6]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "model.rhs = {c: dcdt, L: dLdt}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:410
msgid ""
"We only have boundary conditions on the solvent concentration equation. We "
"must state where a condition is Neumann (on the gradient) or Dirichlet (on "
"the variable itself)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:412
msgid "The boundary condition on the electrode-SEI (x=0) boundary is:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:414
msgid ""
"N|_{\\xi=0} = - R, \\quad  N|_{\\xi=0} = - D(c|_{\\xi=0} )\\left."
"\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial \\xi}\\right|_{\\xi=0}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:419
msgid ""
"which is a Neumann condition. To implement this boundary condition in "
"pybamm, we must first rearrange the equation so that the gradient of the "
"concentration, :math:`\\nabla c|_{x=0}`, is the subject. Therefore we have"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:421
msgid ""
"\\left.\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial \\xi}\\right|_{\\xi=0} = \\frac{R L}"
"{D(c|_{\\xi=0} )}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:426
msgid "which we enter into pybamm as"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[7]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"D_left = pybamm.BoundaryValue(\n"
"    D(c), \"left\"\n"
")  # pybamm requires BoundaryValue(D(c)) and not D(BoundaryValue(c))\n"
"grad_c_left = R * L / D_left"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:450
msgid ""
"On the SEI-electrolyte boundary (:math:`\\xi=1`), we have the boundary "
"condition"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:452
msgid "c|_{\\xi=1} = c_∞"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:466
msgid "which is a Dirichlet condition and is just entered as"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[8]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "c_right = c_inf"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:487
msgid ""
"We now load these boundary conditions into the ``model.boundary_conditions`` "
"dictionary in the following way, being careful to state the type of boundary "
"condition:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[9]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"model.boundary_conditions = {\n"
"    c: {\"left\": (grad_c_left, \"Neumann\"), \"right\": (c_right, "
"\"Dirichlet\")}\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:522
msgid "There are two initial conditions in our model:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:524
msgid "c|_{t=0} = c_∞, \\quad L|_{t=0} = L_0"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:538
msgid "which are simply written in pybamm as"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[10]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"c_init = c_inf\n"
"L_init = L_0"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:560
msgid "and then included into the ``model.initial_conditions`` dictionary:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[11]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "model.initial_conditions = {c: c_init, L: L_init}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:593
msgid ""
"We already have everything required in model for the model to be used and "
"solved, but we have not yet stated what we actually want to output from the "
"model. PyBaMM allows users to output any combination of symbols as an output "
"variable therefore allowing the user the flexibility to output important "
"quantities without further tedious postprocessing steps."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:595
msgid "Some useful outputs for this simple model are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:597
msgid "the SEI thickness"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:598
msgid "the SEI growth rate"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:599
msgid "the solvent concentration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:601
msgid ""
"These are added to the model by adding entries to the ``model.variables`` "
"dictionary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[12]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"model.variables = {\n"
"    \"SEI thickness [m]\": L,\n"
"    \"SEI growth rate [m]\": dLdt,\n"
"    \"Solvent concentration [mol.m-3]\": c,\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:626
msgid ""
"The model is now fully defined and ready to be used. If you plan on reusing "
"the model several times, you can additionally set model defaults which may "
"include: a default geometry to run the model on, a default set of parameter "
"values, a default solver, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:638
msgid "Using the Model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:649
msgid ""
"The model will now behave in the same way as any of the inbuilt PyBaMM "
"models. However, to demonstrate that the model works we display the steps "
"involved in solving the model but we will not go into details within this "
"notebook."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[13]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"# define geometry\n"
"geometry = pybamm.Geometry(\n"
"    {\"SEI layer\": {xi: {\"min\": pybamm.Scalar(0), \"max\": pybamm."
"Scalar(1)}}}\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"def Diffusivity(cc):\n"
"    return cc * 10 ** (-12)\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"# parameter values (not physically based, for example only!)\n"
"param = pybamm.ParameterValues(\n"
"    {\n"
"        \"Reaction rate constant [m.s-1]\": 1e-6,\n"
"        \"Initial thickness [m]\": 1e-6,\n"
"        \"Partial molar volume [m3.mol-1]\": 10,\n"
"        \"Bulk electrolyte solvent concentration [mol.m-3]\": 1,\n"
"        \"Diffusivity [m2.s-1]\": Diffusivity,\n"
"    }\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"# process model and geometry\n"
"param.process_model(model)\n"
"param.process_geometry(geometry)\n"
"\n"
"# mesh and discretise\n"
"submesh_types = {\"SEI layer\": pybamm.Uniform1DSubMesh}\n"
"var_pts = {xi: 100}\n"
"mesh = pybamm.Mesh(geometry, submesh_types, var_pts)\n"
"\n"
"spatial_methods = {\"SEI layer\": pybamm.FiniteVolume()}\n"
"disc = pybamm.Discretisation(mesh, spatial_methods)\n"
"disc.process_model(model)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:703
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"&lt;pybamm.models.base_model.BaseModel at 0x7f3a8005b490&gt;\n"
"</pre>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:709
msgid ""
"\\begin{sphinxVerbatim}[commandchars=\\\\\\{\\}]\n"
"<pybamm.models.base\\_model.BaseModel at 0x7f3a8005b490>\n"
"\\end{sphinxVerbatim}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:716
msgid "<pybamm.models.base_model.BaseModel at 0x7f3a8005b490>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[14]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"# solve\n"
"solver = pybamm.ScipySolver()\n"
"t = [0, 100]  # solve for 100s\n"
"solution = solver.solve(model, t)\n"
"\n"
"# post-process output variables\n"
"L_out = solution[\"SEI thickness [m]\"]\n"
"c_out = solution[\"Solvent concentration [mol.m-3]\"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:744
msgid ""
"Using these outputs, we can now plot the SEI thickness as a function of time "
"and also the solvent concentration profile within the SEI. We use a slider "
"to plot the concentration profile at different times. Note that, even though "
"our model is written in nondimensional form, the processed variables are "
"functions of dimensional space and time (in SI units)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[15]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/6-a-simple-SEI-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n"
"\n"
"# plot SEI thickness in microns as a function of t in microseconds\n"
"# and concentration in mol/m3 as a function of x in microns\n"
"L_0_eval = param.evaluate(L_0)\n"
"xi = np.linspace(0, 1, 100)  # dimensionless space\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"def plot(t):\n"
"    _, (ax1, ax2) = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(10, 5))\n"
"    ax1.plot(solution.t, L_out(solution.t) * 1e6)\n"
"    ax1.plot(t, L_out(t) * 1e6, \"r.\")\n"
"    ax1.set_ylabel(r\"SEI thickness [$\\mu$m]\")\n"
"    ax1.set_xlabel(r\"t [s]\")\n"
"\n"
"    ax2.plot(xi * L_out(t) * 1e6, c_out(t, xi))\n"
"    ax2.set_ylim(0, 1.1)\n"
"    ax2.set_xlim(0, L_out(solution.t[-1]) * 1e6)\n"
"    ax2.set_ylabel(\"Solvent concentration [mol.m-3]\")\n"
"    ax2.set_xlabel(r\"x [$\\mu$m]\")\n"
"\n"
"    plt.tight_layout()\n"
"    plt.show()\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"import ipywidgets as widgets\n"
"\n"
"widgets.interact(\n"
"    plot, t=widgets.FloatSlider(min=0, max=solution.t[-1], step=0.1, "
"value=0)\n"
");"
msgstr ""

